  "summary": "Toledo City Council is considering a broader data privacy and protection ordinance introduced by Councilman Sam Melden that would set rules for data sharing, security, and review of technology contracts going forward. The proposal follows a failed Nov. 4 vote to terminate the city's Flock surveillance camera contracts, and a vote on the Melden proposal is expected Nov. 17.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "If we had this policy in place prior to the procurement of a contract like Flock, we may not be in the situation we're in right now",
      "paragraph_idx": 4
    },
    {
      "quote": "If passed, the Martinez legislation would allow the city to retain its Flock contracts while prohibiting certain uses of the technology, including sharing data related to immigration status, reproductive health care, and gender-affirming care.",
      "paragraph_idx": 9
    },
    {
      "quote": "Mr. Komives is one of five councilmen who sponsored the proposal to terminate the city's Flock contracts and deactivate the cameras, pending the results of an ongoing audit of the city's use of the technology.",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    }
  ],
